# AIR — Airbnb Listing Clone (Teaching Codebase)

This repository is a workshop-grade, production-shaped clone of a vacation-rental
listing page. Every change must read as if a staff engineer is teaching the
"why", not only the "what".

## Non-negotiables

1. Build from scratch. Never scrape, copy, or vendor the reference site's
   JavaScript, CSS, SVG path dumps, or component trees.
2. Visual parity is required (desktop): spacing, type scale, color, hover,
   focus, and motion must match the Airbnb listing language.
3. Strict TypeScript. `any`, leftover `unknown` without narrowing, and
   implicit `any` are defects.
4. Accessibility is a feature, not a polish pass. Modals trap focus. Galleries
   are keyboard-complete. Interactive elements have names.

## TypeScript

- Prefer `interface` for object shapes that may be extended; `type` for unions,
  mapped types, and function signatures.
- Model domain data in `src/types`. Repositories return those types — never
  leak IndexedDB or cache DTOs into React.
- Exhaustive `switch` on discriminated unions (`satisfies never` in default).
- Public functions, hooks, and modules get JSDoc that explains the design
  choice (caching, code-splitting, focus restoration), not a paraphrase of the
  name.

```ts
// ❌ BAD
async function get(id: any) { return fetch('/x/' + id) }

// ✅ GOOD
/** Read-through: Redis mock first so listing navigations do not re-hit IndexedDB. */
async function getListingById(id: ListingId): Promise<Listing> { /* ... */ }
```

## React & Next.js App Router

- Server Components by default. Add `"use client"` only for state, effects,
  browser storage, Framer Motion, or React Query.
- Heavy below-fold UI (reviews, map, photo tour, lightbox) must be loaded with
  `next/dynamic` so the listing hero stays in the critical path.
- Images go through `next/image` (`sizes`, `priority` on LCP, lazy elsewhere).
- Lightbox navigation prefetches adjacent bitmaps before the user presses ←/→.

## Data layer (Repository Pattern)

```
browser                          │ server (src/server, never imported by a client component)
UI → React Query → listing.service → Route Handler → repository → Redis (L1)
                   (typed fetch)     (zod + errors)               → PostgreSQL via Prisma
```

- `src/server/**` is server-only. A client component importing it is a defect;
  `server-only` makes it a build error.
- Repositories return domain types. Prisma models stop at `mappers.ts` and
  never reach React.
- PrismaClient hangs off `globalThis`. Next bundles each route separately,
  so a module-level `let` would open a pool per route.
- Redis is a separate process (Compose, Upstash, or ioredis). The in-memory
  adapter is only the fallback when no URL is configured.
- React Query owns stale-while-revalidate and the optimistic wishlist update.
- Availability is never served stale (`staleTime: 0`, `Cache-Control: no-store`).
  Every other read may be.

## Money and writes

- Never trust a price from the client. `src/lib/pricing.ts` is shared so the
  booking card can preview a total, but the server recomputes it from the
  stored nightly rate before writing.
- Availability check and booking insert share one Serializable transaction.
  A GiST exclusion constraint is the database's copy of the same rule.
  Checking outside the transaction is a double-sell bug.
- Idempotent writes use PUT (the wishlist sends desired state, not a toggle).
- Validate at the route with zod; repositories take already-valid domain input.
- Error codes are a closed union (`not_found`, `invalid_request`,
  `dates_unavailable`, `internal_error`) so the client can branch type-safely.
  A conflict is 409, a bad body is 422.

## Tailwind

- Use design tokens from `tailwind.config.ts` (`airbnb-rausch`, `airbnb-hof`).
- No magic hex in JSX except as a last resort; prefer tokens.
- Prefer spacing scale that maps to Airbnb's 8px grid (and 4px photo gaps).
- No `@apply` of large visual blocks — keep utilities in the markup so the
  workshop can see the layout without hunting CSS files.
- Desktop-only: do not add a mobile breakpoint redesign. `min-w` the shell.

## Accessibility

- Icon-only controls need `aria-label`.
- Dialogs: `role="dialog"`, `aria-modal="true"`, labelled by title id.
- Focus trap + restore on close. Escape closes. Lightbox: ←/→.
- Visible `focus-visible` rings (`2px #222`, offset 2px). Never `outline-none`
  without a replacement.
- Prefer semantic HTML (`button`, `nav`, `main`, headings in order).

## Motion

- Framer Motion for overlay enter/exit, lightbox crossfade, and scroll-linked
  header elevation. Match Airbnb easing: cubic-bezier(0.2, 0, 0, 1).
- Honor `prefers-reduced-motion`: skip non-essential transforms.

## Comments & structure

- File-level JSDoc describes the architectural role of the module.
- Colocate component-specific hooks next to the component when they are not
  reused. Shared hooks live in `src/hooks`.
- One component, one job. Photo grid does not own lightbox state machines
  beyond callbacks.

## What not to do

- Do not call `document.querySelector` to fake React state.
- Do not put secrets in the client bundle (this mock has none; keep it that way).
- Do not add libraries when the platform (Next, RQ, Prisma, Redis, Framer) already covers it.
